  "notes": "The Characters / Songbook — artist-development-led adult R&B, 1990–2010. The band is \"66–92; ballads 66–80 half-time\", so the ballad half is the base with the mode at 66, the one sourced tempo point. Three drum deltas from a description that is itself about subordination: \"soft programmed kit with a live-feel snare on beat 3 of the doubled grid, brushed hats, tambourine; **drums subordinate to the vocal by design**\", marked `[LOW-CONF]`. So the snare sits 4–10 ms off the grid with real ghost activity (live-feel), the hat lane is an 8th base at 0.07 fill density (brushed), and the percussion is a downbeat tambourine/shaker/rim bed at −9 dB with `tambourineMirrorsClap` true; `quantizeStrength` is 0.62. The identifying parameter is not a sound but a workflow, and the research quotes it: his method is **vocal-first and development-first** — \"Most producers try to produce her when you cannot do that. You simply have to just let her do what she does. Then you critique it, fix it\" — and \"he prioritises songwriting substance over production display\". That is encoded where it can be: the melody has the widest density range and the second-widest register of the ballad producers here (MIDI 52–86, 3–10 notes a bar), the arrangement carries long verses (8–16 bars), a real bridge and an extended ad-lib tag, and the chord bed is authored as four-chord cycles \"built to sit under a big lead\" rather than as a hook. ⚠ Confidence is **low** and the research says why: \"philosophy and credits sourced; drum/harmony detail is board-level and marked\", with the key claim, the drum claim and the bass claim all carrying `[LOW-CONF]`. ⚠ Every velocity, density and register number is INFERRED. ⚠ `session.keys` is INFERRED: the entry's Keys line gives \"minor-mode with gospel lifts [LOW-CONF]\" and enumerates no tonic, so the values here and their weights are this author's reading of that line.",
